# dp_exchange_webull — environment variable template.
#
# This is the ONLY .env file that is committed. `.gitignore` carries
# `.env*` / `!.env.sample`; every other .env is yours and stays local.
#
#     cp .env.sample .env
#
# Secrets live in .env files and nowhere else. There is no `config/*.secret.exs` and no
# Vault layer.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This repo holds no Webull credential, and that is deliberate
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# It does NOT obtain, store or refresh one. The host does. This package signs a request
# when a caller hands it credentials and never keeps a copy beyond the call — see
# `DpExchange.Webull.Credentials`, which additionally keeps a credential held in feed
# state out of a crash report.
#
# **This venue signs every request.** `capabilities/0` declares
# `credential_benefit: :required`: there is no anonymous quote endpoint, and the scheme is
# an `app_key` / `app_secret` pair, with `x-signature` computed as
# `HMAC-SHA1(app_secret <> "&", encoded_str)` — see `DpExchange.Webull.Auth`. That is
# unlike Gemini, which offers an API key pair *and* an OAuth 2.0 flow; do not carry
# assumptions between the two packages.
#
# So there is no App Key here, and its absence is not a claim that none is needed. Tier-1
# tests use in-process fakes and tier-2 tests hit public endpoints only, so nothing in this
# repo has a credential to do anything with — and an unused credential in a public repo is
# a liability with no upside.
#
# `environment: :uat` needs a credential too. It is authenticated REST against test data,
# and it has **no broker at all** — `mqtt-uat.webullbroker.com` does not resolve — so a
# feed there refuses to stream rather than silently falling back. See
# `DpExchange.Webull.Environment`.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Publishing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Organisation key. CI publishes with the org secret; this is only for publishing by hand.
HEX_API_KEY=<your-hex-api-key>
